::ENG3_U04_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: An article explains that school murals preserve local history by showing student interviews, neighborhood scenes, and captions for important places. Which statement best names the subject? {
~The subject is that students paint things.#This is too narrow and informal.
=The subject is how school murals can preserve local history.#Correct. This names the subject accurately.
~The subject is captions and paint colors.#This focuses on details but misses the larger subject.
~The subject is that art is always better than writing.#This adds an unsupported comparison.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: An essay explains community gardens through shared tools, elders teaching planting methods, and students recording recipes. Which claim best shows subject understanding? {
~The essay is about gardens being nice places.#This is vague and does not show subject understanding.
~The essay has three examples about plants.#This notices examples but misses the subject.
=The essay presents community gardens as spaces that meet practical needs and preserve cultural memory.#Correct. This accurately captures the subject and meaning.
~The essay proves everyone should garden.#This overstates the text.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Draft: The article shows the mural is important. Which revision uses more precise academic vocabulary? {
~The mural is really meaningful to people.#This remains vague.
~The mural does a lot for the school.#This is informal and imprecise.
~The mural is a cool example of history.#This is informal.
=The mural functions as a public archive of neighborhood history.#Correct. This uses precise academic vocabulary.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: Libraries do a lot of stuff for people. Which revision best improves clarity and style? {
=Public libraries provide multiple civic services, including access to information, job support, and community meeting space.#Correct. This revision is specific and academic.
~Libraries help people with stuff and things.#This is still vague.
~Libraries are good because people use them.#This is too general.
~Libraries are places that have books and other stuff.#This is informal and unclear.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: A report uses headings for books, internet access, job help, and meeting rooms before arguing that libraries are civic infrastructure. Which analysis connects subject and structure best? {
~The report has headings about library services.#This is summary only.
~Libraries have books and meeting rooms.#This lists details.
~The report is organized in a helpful way.#This is too vague.
=The headings separate the library's services so readers can understand how each one supports the report's larger subject of civic infrastructure.#Correct. This connects organization to subject understanding.
}
